<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-engineering/foundational/water-chemistry-and-microbiology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Environmental engineering's water work rests on two sciences: <strong>chemistry</strong>
    (what is dissolved in the water, and what will react) and <strong>microbiology</strong>
    (who is living in it, and what they eat). Every treatment plant on Earth is an arrangement
    of tanks that exploits exactly these two: chemistry to remove chemicals, biology to remove
    biodegradable matter, and disinfection — chemistry acting on biology — to finish the
    pathogens. A beginner looking at a plant sees concrete and pipes; an environmental engineer
    sees a set of controlled equilibria, engineered reaction rates and a cultivated microbial
    ecosystem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does this matter beyond the plant? Because water is the medium through which civilisation
    ships both its life support and its diseases. Cholera, typhoid and infant diarrhoeal death
    were tamed not primarily by medicine but by water treatment — the filtration and
    chlorination revolutions of the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries are credited
    with roughly half the total mortality reduction in American cities of that era. The same
    sciences also decide whether a pipe corrodes lead into a child's glass of water (Flint,
    Michigan, 2014: a chemistry failure — untreated river water stripped the protective scale
    inside the pipes), whether a river's ammonia kills its fish, and whether a reservoir's
    algae bloom poisons a city's supply. Equilibrium, kinetics and microbes are not an
    academic preamble; they are the safety system.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="equilibrium, kinetics, bugs and the CT rule" widget="equationStep" config={chemistryCfg} />

  <h2>Equilibrium chemistry and pH</h2>
  <p>
    Chemical equilibrium answers the question "where does this reaction settle?" — and in water
    almost everything settles, because water is the solvent reactions happen in. The formalism
    is the equilibrium constant: for a reaction A ⇌ B, the ratio of products to reactants at
    equilibrium is fixed by thermodynamics, so push one side and the other responds — Le
    Chatelier's principle, the workhorse intuition. <strong>Acid–base equilibrium</strong> is
    the master case. Acids donate protons, and the strength of the donation is an equilibrium
    constant whose logarithm is the familiar pKa; pH is the negative logarithm of the hydrogen
    ion activity, and the two connect through Henderson–Hasselbalch arithmetic. The practical
    consequence: the <em>form</em> a chemical takes in water — and therefore its toxicity, its
    solubility and its disinfecting power — is a function of pH.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>carbonate buffer system</strong> is nature's own pH control and the model
    example. Atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> dissolves in water, becomes carbonic acid, and
    equilibrates through bicarbonate to carbonate; together with the alkalinity contributed by
    dissolved rock (limestone dissolved by that same acid), the system holds most natural waters
    between pH 6 and 9 and resists change — <em>buffering</em>. The same system decides whether
    water deposits <strong>scale</strong> (calcium carbonate precipitating onto pipes —
    protective, mildly annoying) or <strong>corrodes</strong> them (aggressive, undersaturated
    water dissolving the calcium carbonate cement and, catastrophically, the lead in old
    service lines). The <strong>Langelier saturation index</strong> is just this equilibrium
    arithmetic packaged as a number: positive means scaling water, negative means corrosive
    water — and corrosion control, the discipline that keeps lead out of drinking water, is
    the art of nudging the carbonate chemistry (plus orthophosphate inhibitors that form a
    protective mineral film) until the index and the pipe agree.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example shows why pH is the field's master variable. Free chlorine in water exists
    as two species in equilibrium: hypochlorous acid (HOCl, potent) and hypochlorite ion
    (OCl<sup>−</sup>, weak), with pKa ≈ 7.5. At pH 7.5 the split is roughly 50–50; at pH 8.5
    about 90 per cent has shifted to the weak form — the same chlorine dose buys a fraction of
    the kill. This single equilibrium explains why regulators' CT tables demand far more
    contact time at higher pH, why plants control chlorination pH deliberately, and why "pH"
    is the first number an operator checks after chlorine itself. Speciation logic repeats
    everywhere: ammonia is benign as NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> and toxic to fish as
    NH<sub>3</sub>, and the fraction shifts with pH; hydrogen sulphide smells as H<sub>2</sub>S
    and vanishes as HS<sup>−</sup>; cyanide is more lethal as the undissociated acid. In water,
    asking "how much?" is always the second question — the first is "in what form?"
  </p>

  <h2>Kinetics: the clock that chemistry runs on</h2>
  <p>
    Equilibrium says where a reaction ends; <strong>kinetics</strong> says how fast it gets
    there — and in treatment design, speed is the resource, because tanks cost money per cubic
    metre and the water must leave them. Environmental kinetics lives on the first-order
    reaction: a rate proportional to how much remains, N = N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup>, which
    yields the intuitive <strong>half-life</strong>, t<sub>½</sub> = 0.693/k — the time for
    half of anything to decay. Pollutant degradation, pathogen die-off, radioactive decay and
    biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) exertion all follow this shape; a pollutant with a 4-day
    half-life is a lake management problem, one with a 4-hour half-life is a plant design
    problem, and one with a 40-year half-life (PFAS, famously) is a regulation problem. Rates
    climb with temperature — classically roughly doubling per 10 °C rise — which is why
    disinfection CT requirements are tabulated by temperature and why biological treatment
    slows painfully in winter (nitrifying bacteria, the most cold-sensitive of the workforce,
    essentially stop below about 10 °C).
  </p>
  <p>
    Disinfection kinetics is the unit's showcase application. <strong>Chick's law</strong>
    (Harriette Chick, 1908) observed that microorganisms die off exponentially under a fixed
    disinfectant concentration — first-order again; <strong>Chick–Watson</strong> folded the
    concentration dependence in, making the kill depend on the product of concentration and
    time — the <strong>CT value</strong>, in mg·min/L. CT is the design currency of drinking
    water disinfection: regulators publish, for each pathogen (or indicator), each disinfectant,
    each pH and temperature, the CT needed per log of inactivation, and the engineer's job is
    to guarantee the plant's contactor delivers it. The calculator below runs the logic: at
    fixed CT, raising the pH strips inactivation because the HOCl equilibrium has been pushed
    against the engineer. One refinement makes it real: real contactors have <em>baffling
    factors</em> — some water short-circuits the tank and leaves early — so the effective
    contact time is the theoretical residence time multiplied by a baffling factor (roughly
    0.3 for a poor unbaffled basin, approaching 0.7 for a well-baffled serpentine one), and
    the design response is geometry: add baffles, buy time. Kinetics also runs the biological
    reactors: solids retention time (how long microbes stay) is a kinetic dial that selects
    which organisms the plant farms, and flocculation basins are designed around the product
    of velocity gradient and time (Gt) because particle collisions — the mechanism of
    coagulation — are, once again, rate-limited.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="CT-based inactivation under pH and dose" widget="sliderLab" config={disinfectionCfg} />

  <h2>Waterborne microorganisms: pathogens, indicators and the workforce</h2>
  <p>
    The microbiology has two faces: the enemies and the workforce. The <strong>pathogens</strong>
    span three size classes. Bacteria: <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> (cholera), <em>Salmonella
    typhi</em> (typhoid), <em>Shigella</em>, pathogenic <em>E. coli</em> (O157:H7),
    <em>Campylobacter</em>, <em>Legionella</em> (the plumbing-dweller behind legionnaires'
    disease, which water treatment must fight in buildings, not just plants). Viruses:
    norovirus, rotavirus, hepatitis A — tiny, chlorination-resistant compared with bacteria,
    and prolific shedders (billions of particles per gram of faeces). Protozoa: <strong>Giardia</strong>
    and <strong>Cryptosporidium</strong>, chlorine-resistant oocysts that define the top of the
    regulatory bar — <em>Cryptosporidium</em> infamously broke through Milwaukee's supply in
    1993, sickening an estimated 400,000 people and killing over a hundred, and forced the
    filtration-and-turbidity regime now required everywhere. Testing for each pathogen directly
    is slow and expensive, so regulation rides on <strong>indicators</strong>: total coliforms
    and especially <em>E. coli</em>, faecal organisms that travel with the pathogens but are
    cheap and quick to detect. The indicator approach is a bet — indicator absent, pathogens
    probably absent — and its known weakness (viruses and protozoa survive differently than
    bacteria) is why modern rules layer multiple indicators plus CT credit plus filtration
    performance instead of trusting one number.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>workforce</strong> side is the quieter revolution of the twentieth century:
    modern wastewater treatment is deliberately cultivated microbial ecology. In the
    <strong>activated sludge</strong> process, aerated tanks hold a dense mixed community of
    bacteria and protozoa that consume dissolved and suspended organic matter, forming flocs
    that settle out — the engineer manages food-to-microorganism ratio, oxygen and solids
    retention time the way a farmer manages feed and stock, and an ecology crash (bulking
    filaments, toxicity shocks) shows up as a failing effluent. <strong>Nitrification</strong>
    farms the slower autotrophs that oxidise ammonia to nitrate — protecting fish and
    preventing ammonia demand from consuming the receiving stream's oxygen — and
    <strong>denitrification</strong> then farms anaerobes that convert nitrate to nitrogen gas,
    completing biological nitrogen removal. Enhanced biological phosphorus removal exploits
    polyphosphate-accumulating organisms' "luxury uptake"; and in the anaerobic digester, a
    third consortium converts the settled sludge to biogas (roughly 60 per cent methane) —
    sewage works making their own fuel. The treatment train from screening to disinfection is
    thus physical, chemical and biological stages interleaved, each tuned by the two sciences
    this page teaches.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that disinfection means sterilisation — that treatment eliminates pathogens. It does not and cannot; regulation speaks in <strong>log reductions</strong>: a 3-log process removes 99.9 per cent, 4-log removes 99.99 per cent. The targets are set by risk arithmetic (how many survivors can the dose-response tolerate), and the multi-barrier design — watershed protection, filtration, disinfection, distribution integrity — exists precisely because no single barrier reaches zero. Zero is a reserve held in depth, not a claim.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The frontier problems are extensions of these three ideas. <strong>Disinfection by-products</strong>
    are a genuinely two-sided equilibrium problem: chlorine is a superb biocide but reacts with
    natural organic matter to form trihalomethanes and haloacetic acids — regulated carcinogen
    candidates — so the engineer trades pathogen risk against chemical risk, managing the
    balance with precursor removal (coagulation, activated carbon), chloramines (less potent,
    but slower-degrading and DBP-light) and ozone-plus-biofiltration. <strong>Emerging
    contaminants</strong> — PFAS "forever chemicals", pharmaceuticals, microplastics — stress
    the kinetic frame: their environmental half-lives are so long that treatment must be
    destructive (advanced oxidation) or separative (membranes, activated carbon), and
    regulation is racing the science. <strong>Membrane treatment</strong> rewrites removal as
    a sieving problem but inherits a kinetics problem — fouling, the microbial and chemical
    clogging of membrane surfaces, which is now the central cost driver of advanced treatment.
    And the oldest microbial problem is returning with new urgency: <strong>antibiotic
    resistance</strong> spreads through water systems (wastewater plants as selection and
    exchange sites), making the microbial ecology of treatment plants a public-health
    surveillance subject, not just an engineering one. Climate stress raises every stake:
    warmer water slows treatment kinetics, intensifies blooms, and pushes salinity into
    intakes. The discipline's through-line, though, is unchanged since the first sand filter:
    equilibria, rates and microbes — understand the chemistry, respect the clock, farm the
    biology — and the water is safe.
  </p>
  <p>
    One closing application shows the two sciences collaborating inside a single tank.
    <strong>Coagulation</strong> — the dosing of alum or ferric salts ahead of settling — is
    equilibrium chemistry and microbial strategy at once: the metal salt hydrolyses through a
    cascade of charged species (whose forms the pH fixes — speciation logic again), those
    species neutralise the negative surface charges that keep colloidal clay and organic
    particles repelling one another, and the destabilised particles collide and aggregate
    through the velocity-gradient kinetics of the flocculation basin. The same dose then does
    microbial work: coagulation sweeps out much of the natural organic matter that would
    otherwise become disinfection by-products, and strips the turbidity that would shelter
    <em>Cryptosporidium</em> from UV and chlorine alike — which is why regulators credit
    filtration performance, and the coagulation chemistry behind it, as pathogen barriers in
    their own right. The operator's jar test, run routinely in most plants, is this whole
    paragraph compressed into fifteen minutes: dose a beaker series, watch the floc form and
    settle, pick the chemistry — and the plant's equilibria, kinetics and ecology all follow
    from that small, daily act of applied science.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Environmental specifications; Benjamin &amp; Lawler, <em>Water Quality Engineering</em>; Bitton, <em>Wastewater Microbiology</em>.</p>
